1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 f6 3. Nxe5 Qe7 (3... fxe5 { Now black steps into the trap. The variations that follow can lead to a winning advantage for white. } 4. Qh5+ g6 { This is the "best" move for white, but it still leads to serious material loss. } (4... Ke7 { This is the "wrong" move, it will end (except one escape with big loss for black) into mate. } 5. Qxe5+ Kf7 6. Bc4+ d5 (6... Kg6 7. Qf5+ Kh6 8. d4+ g5 9. h4 Be7 10. hxg5+ Kg7 11. Qf7#) 7. Bxd5+ Kg6 8. h4 h6 9. Bxb7 Bxb7 { Mate is inevitable. } (9... Bd6 { This is the only way for black to prevent mate. } 10. Qb5 (10. Qa5 Bxb7 11. Qf5#) 10... Nf6 11. Bxa8 Na6 12. Bb7) 10. Qf5#) 5. Qxe5+ { [%cal Ge5e8,Ge5h8] } 5... Qe7 6. Qxh8 Qxe4+ 7. Kd1 Qe6 (7... Qf4 8. f3 (8. Qxg8 Qxf2 (8... Qf7 9. Qxf7+ Kxf7) 9. Bb5 Nc6 10. Rf1 Qd4 11. Qxf8#) 8... Qf7 9. Qe5+) (7... Ne7 8. Nc3 Qf5 9. f3 d5 10. Qxh7) 8. Qxh7 Ne7 9. Qh8 d5) 4. Nf3 d5 (4... Qxe4+ 5. Be2 d5 6. O-O Ne7 7. Re1) 5. d3 dxe4 6. dxe4 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 $14 { Although this line does not lead to a real trap, white can still use its development speed as an advantage. } *


