[Event "Openings: White 1. e4 g3 - d4 Bg7 - Robatsch (Modern) Defense"]
[Site "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AtNlFBWBWPw"]
[Date "2020.11.21"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B06"]
[Opening "Modern Defense: Standard Defense"]
[StudyName "Openings"]
[ChapterName "White 1. e4 g3 - d4 Bg7 - Robatsch (Modern) Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/sNPxVVfS/U4AP7TG2"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/Bijbrengen"]
[UTCDate "2020.11.21"]
[UTCTime "18:32:31"]

1. e4 g6 { Black does not care to not occupy the center but wants to have the a1 to h8 diagonal to put pressure on the center from the sides. } 2. d4 { So white uses the opportunity to further occupy the center. } 2... Bg7 { The Fianchetto is a fact. } 3. Nc3 { Protect the pawns } 3... d6 4. Be3 { Protect d4 against Knight on c6. } 4... c6 { Although black can bring it's Queen out, it is more likely that he goes for b5. } 5. a4 { Prevent black to occupy space on the Queen side. } 5... Nf6 { Opposite castling seems to occur. The black knight on f6 threatens to go to g4. This could be stoppen by h3 or with f3. The first has the advantage of not blocking development of the knight g1. The second that it protects the central pawn. } 6. f3 O-O { To reach to the black King at some point in the match we need to capture the Bishop on g7. } 7. Qd2 { Black tries to distract the threat on h6. } 7... e5 { Be careful not to go to h6. The pawn on d4 will be lost. } { [%csl Gd4] } 8. Nge2 { The pawn is protected but of course the white bishop on f1 is blocked. } 8... exd4 9. Nxd4 { Black should continuing distracting by playing d5. } 9... d5 { Taking means the center will open on the King file. } 10. e5 Nfd7 { We can push the pawn because our knight can fork on e6. } 11. e6 { He has to move the knight and that means we can get his f7 pawn, one of the three pawns defending the king } { [%cal Ge6f7,Ge6d7,Rf7e6] } 11... Ne5 12. exf7+ Rxf7 { He takes with the rook to keep it's knight in the center. The problem for white is that the e file is open. } 13. O-O-O { The fact that the pawn is out, is not really a problem because it is defended and you can not go through the hole in the defense easily by playing Kb1. } 13... c5 { We can put the knight back because the situation is different then before. The fork threatening pawn on d5 ihas the queen behind it which has a double attack by the white queen and rook. So when the pawn takes, the black queen is captured. When looking at the position it seems white has the advantage because it pieces are better developed. } 14. Nb3 { [%cal Gd4c3,Gd4e3,Rd2d8] } 14... c4 { Keep the knight active and keep the pressure on the d5 pawn. } 15. Nc5 { [%cal Gc5e4] } 15... b6 16. N5e4 Be6 { Black tries to defend it's pawn although it can still be captured. It woukld be better if the bishop would have moved to b7. Because now the bishop and rook are connected diagional and that offers the opportunity for a knight to make a fork. } 17. Ng5 Re7 18. Nxe6 Rxe6 19. Qxd5 (19. Nxd5 Nd3+ 20. Bxd3 cxd3 21. Qxd3 Qc8 22. Bd4 { We have to remove the black bishop } 22... Bxd4 23. Qxd4 { He has to develop } 23... Re2 24. Rd2 Qe6 25. Nc7 Nc6 26. Nxe6 Nxd4 27. Nxd4 Rxd2 28. Kxd2 Rd8 29. c3 Kf7 30. f4 Re8 31. g4) 19... Qxd5 20. Nxd5 $16 *


